1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,996
Take the lift or the stairs,
you reach the same floor.

2
00:00:03,996 --> 00:00:08,727
Pay a hundred rupees at once, or fifty
and fifty, the total is the same.

3
00:00:09,127 --> 00:00:10,535
The road can change.

4
00:00:10,535 --> 00:00:13,114
The start and the end decide the answer.

5
00:00:13,564 --> 00:00:15,693
Aarav pushes his bicycle up a hill.

6
00:00:15,693 --> 00:00:17,365
The straight road is steep.

7
00:00:17,365 --> 00:00:18,949
The winding road is long.

8
00:00:19,299 --> 00:00:21,507
Either way, he gains the same height.

9
00:00:22,007 --> 00:00:23,970
Chemistry has a hill like this.

10
00:00:23,970 --> 00:00:28,141
By the end, you will find the heat
of a reaction nobody can measure.

11
00:00:28,791 --> 00:00:30,424
Here is the problem.

12
00:00:30,424 --> 00:00:34,819
Burn carbon in plenty of oxygen,
and you get carbon dioxide.

13
00:00:34,819 --> 00:00:36,167
Easy to measure.

14
00:00:36,517 --> 00:00:38,004
But carbon monoxide?

15
00:00:38,004 --> 00:00:42,996
Burn carbon in a little oxygen, and
you always get a mix of both gases.

16
00:00:43,396 --> 00:00:48,068
So the heat for making pure carbon
monoxide cannot be measured directly.

17
00:00:48,068 --> 00:00:49,642
We need another road.

18
00:00:50,242 --> 00:00:53,191
Let's walk both roads
on our molecule bench.

19
00:00:53,191 --> 00:00:55,454
At the top, carbon and oxygen.

20
00:00:55,804 --> 00:00:57,447
Road one, straight down.

21
00:00:57,447 --> 00:00:59,704
Carbon burns fully to carbon dioxide.

22
00:00:59,704 --> 00:01:02,475
Minus three ninety three
point five kilojoules.

23
00:01:02,875 --> 00:01:04,534
Road two has two steps.

24
00:01:04,534 --> 00:01:07,022
First, carbon becomes carbon monoxide.

25
00:01:07,022 --> 00:01:08,778
This is the unknown step.

26
00:01:09,178 --> 00:01:12,261
Then carbon monoxide
burns to carbon dioxide.

27
00:01:12,261 --> 00:01:14,108
That one we can measure.

28
00:01:14,108 --> 00:01:15,840
Minus two eighty three.

29
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:20,133
Both roads start at the same
place and end at the same place.

30
00:01:20,133 --> 00:01:22,547
So both must drop by the same total.

31
00:01:23,147 --> 00:01:24,351
This is Hess's law.

32
00:01:24,351 --> 00:01:26,706
Germain Hess stated it in eighteen forty.

33
00:01:27,056 --> 00:01:32,141
If a reaction happens in steps, its
enthalpy change is the sum of the enthalpy

34
00:01:32,141 --> 00:01:33,723
changes of the steps.

35
00:01:34,123 --> 00:01:36,866
It works because enthalpy
is a state function.

36
00:01:36,866 --> 00:01:41,222
Like height on a hill, it depends
only on the start and the end, not the

37
00:01:41,222 --> 00:01:42,122
road.

38
00:01:42,379 --> 00:01:44,363
Now, solve the unknown step.

39
00:01:44,363 --> 00:01:45,408
Step by step.

40
00:01:45,758 --> 00:01:46,658
Step one.

41
00:01:46,564 --> 00:01:48,331
Road one equals road two.

42
00:01:48,331 --> 00:01:52,927
Minus three ninety three point five
equals x plus minus two eighty three.

43
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:54,577
Step two.

44
00:01:54,456 --> 00:01:56,260
Move the known step across.

45
00:01:56,260 --> 00:02:00,363
x equals minus three ninety three
point five plus two eighty three.

46
00:02:01,113 --> 00:02:02,013
Step three.

47
00:02:01,942 --> 00:02:05,375
x is minus one hundred ten
point five kilojoules per mole.

48
00:02:05,375 --> 00:02:07,546
The heat for making carbon monoxide.

49
00:02:08,296 --> 00:02:11,658
To add steps like this, we
follow three rules for equations.

50
00:02:12,008 --> 00:02:12,908
Rule one.

51
00:02:12,834 --> 00:02:16,412
Flip an equation, and flip
the sign of its enthalpy.

52
00:02:16,412 --> 00:02:19,003
Walking up a hill undoes walking down.

53
00:02:19,403 --> 00:02:20,303
Rule two.

54
00:02:20,246 --> 00:02:23,939
Multiply an equation by two,
and its enthalpy doubles.

55
00:02:24,339 --> 00:02:25,239
Rule three.

56
00:02:25,077 --> 00:02:27,254
Add equations, and add their enthalpies.

57
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:29,559
Write every state, gas, liquid or solid.

58
00:02:30,159 --> 00:02:32,800
Now compare the two roads side by side.

59
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,318
Road one, one step.

60
00:02:34,318 --> 00:02:35,886
Road two, two steps.

61
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:37,366
Pause and think.

62
00:02:37,366 --> 00:02:39,763
Which road gives out more heat in total?

63
00:02:42,963 --> 00:02:43,863
Neither!

64
00:02:43,579 --> 00:02:46,210
Both give minus three
ninety three point five.

65
00:02:46,210 --> 00:02:48,486
The road changes, the total does not.

66
00:02:49,136 --> 00:02:50,282
Remember this.

67
00:02:50,282 --> 00:02:54,837
Same start, same end, same
enthalpy change, whatever the road.

68
00:02:55,537 --> 00:02:57,227
Now, one worked example.

69
00:02:57,527 --> 00:03:00,385
Find the enthalpy of formation of methane.

70
00:03:00,385 --> 00:03:03,027
Carbon plus two hydrogen gives methane.

71
00:03:03,027 --> 00:03:05,039
We only know burning values.

72
00:03:05,539 --> 00:03:08,878
Carbon burns, minus three
ninety three point five.

73
00:03:08,878 --> 00:03:13,200
Hydrogen burns to liquid water,
minus two eighty five point eight.

74
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:16,278
Methane burns, minus
eight ninety point three.

75
00:03:17,028 --> 00:03:18,812
Keep carbon's burning as it is.

76
00:03:18,812 --> 00:03:21,449
Double hydrogen's, because
we need two hydrogen.

77
00:03:21,449 --> 00:03:23,360
Minus five seventy one point six.

78
00:03:24,110 --> 00:03:27,225
Flip methane's burning, because
methane must be a product.

79
00:03:27,225 --> 00:03:28,933
Plus eight ninety point three.

80
00:03:29,683 --> 00:03:30,647
Add them up.

81
00:03:30,647 --> 00:03:34,830
Minus three ninety three point five,
minus five seventy one point six,

82
00:03:34,830 --> 00:03:36,748
plus eight ninety point three.

83
00:03:37,498 --> 00:03:40,368
Minus seventy four point
eight kilojoules per mole.

84
00:03:40,368 --> 00:03:42,476
Water and carbon dioxide cancel out.

85
00:03:43,126 --> 00:03:44,588
Now, the exam trap.

86
00:03:44,938 --> 00:03:48,151
Students flip an equation
but forget to flip the sign.

87
00:03:48,151 --> 00:03:51,364
Or they double an equation
but keep the same enthalpy.

88
00:03:51,764 --> 00:03:56,093
Every change you make to the
equation, make it to the enthalpy too.

89
00:03:56,743 --> 00:03:58,015
Where is this asked?

90
00:03:58,015 --> 00:03:59,501
Boards ask the statement.

91
00:03:59,501 --> 00:04:03,411
Entrance tests give two or three
equations and ask you to combine them.

92
00:04:04,011 --> 00:04:05,348
Exam level now.

93
00:04:05,348 --> 00:04:09,586
Sulphur burns to sulphur
dioxide, minus two ninety seven.

94
00:04:09,586 --> 00:04:14,820
Two sulphur dioxide plus oxygen give
two sulphur trioxide, minus one ninety

95
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:15,720
eight.

96
00:04:15,511 --> 00:04:19,692
Find the enthalpy for sulphur
making one sulphur trioxide.

97
00:04:19,692 --> 00:04:21,085
Pause and solve.

98
00:04:26,485 --> 00:04:27,600
The answer is C.

99
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:29,544
Minus three ninety six kilojoules.

100
00:04:30,044 --> 00:04:34,562
We need only one sulphur trioxide,
so halve the second equation.

101
00:04:34,562 --> 00:04:35,990
Minus ninety nine.

102
00:04:36,490 --> 00:04:38,123
Add the first equation.

103
00:04:38,123 --> 00:04:41,291
Minus two ninety seven,
plus minus ninety nine.

104
00:04:41,791 --> 00:04:43,471
Minus three ninety six.

105
00:04:43,471 --> 00:04:44,895
A forgot to halve.

106
00:04:45,495 --> 00:04:47,035
Here is your recap.

107
00:04:47,035 --> 00:04:48,618
Screenshot this card.

108
00:04:51,818 --> 00:04:54,004
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

109
00:04:54,404 --> 00:04:55,304
One.

110
00:04:54,799 --> 00:04:56,509
State Hess's law in one sentence.

111
00:04:57,309 --> 00:04:58,209
Two.

112
00:04:57,768 --> 00:05:01,468
Why can't we measure the heat of
making carbon monoxide directly?

113
00:05:02,268 --> 00:05:03,168
Three.

114
00:05:02,775 --> 00:05:05,773
What happens to the enthalpy
when you flip an equation?

115
00:05:06,673 --> 00:05:11,450
Next time: why does ice melt on its
own, even though it takes heat in?

116
00:05:11,450 --> 00:05:14,030
We find out in entropy and spontaneity.
